HDFC Bank's reported plan to move operational and back-end staff to customer-facing roles raises several important questions.

If this initiative is being introduced in the name of AI, productivity, and organizational efficiency, then employees, investors, and other stakeholders deserve clarity.

My questions are:

• What will be the objective and transparent criteria for selecting employees for these role changes?

• Will employees' consent or individual circumstances be considered before such transfers?

• How many senior management personnel will be included in this exercise, or will it apply primarily to junior and mid-level staff?

• How will "productivity" be defined? Will it be measured solely on sales performance, or will operational excellence, customer service, compliance, and risk management also be considered?

• Will the CEO, Mr. Sashi Jagdishan, and the senior leadership disclose the productivity metrics by which their own performance is evaluated?

If this exercise is genuinely about transformation, the process should be transparent, objective, and applied consistently across all levels of the organization.

If, however, the process lacks clear criteria and independent oversight, it raises concerns that it could become a workforce rationalization exercise in which employees perceived as less favored are moved to customer-facing roles and later assessed primarily on productivity metrics.

Employees, shareholders, and regulators deserve complete transparency on how this initiative will be implemented.

